From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: virtudude <virtudude@protonmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:06:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] rtc: qcom-tad: add RTC alarm support through the SoCCP TAD
 service

On Glymur laptops the PMIC RTC alarm interrupt belongs to the SoCCP
battery-manager firmware, so rtc-pm8xxx cannot request it (-EPERM) and
has no alarm. The firmware exposes the ACPI Time and Alarm Device
service over pmic_glink instead, but on the HP OmniBook Ultra 14
(SOCCP.FW.2.1) it answers only the legacy TAD opcodes 0x60-0x65 on the
RTC owner (32784), not the 0x68/0x69 the proposed rtc-glink driver
uses, so that driver times out at probe.

Add a driver for the pmic_glink "rtc-glink" auxiliary device. It reads
the time from the GIO service's TAD_RT block (owner 32785, 0x30200), as
the Windows ACPI TAD methods do, with GIO alerts left off so the
battery manager and UCSI notifications keep flowing. It arms both the
AC and the DC TAD timer, as the firmware only wakes for the one
matching the power source. On ALARM_EXPIRED (0x6a) it disarms both and
reports the alarm, also as a hard wakeup event: the GLINK interrupt is
IRQF_NO_SUSPEND but not a wakeup interrupt, so s2idle would otherwise
go back to sleep.

The firmware sometimes answers _STV with status 0x200 and keeps failing
on an immediate resend, so, as a heuristic, such an _STV disables the
timer and is retried after 50, 100 and 200 ms. There is no set_time, as
writing TAD_RT updates the firmware's RTC bookkeeping.

Tested on HP OmniBook Ultra 14: alarms up to 20 minutes resume from
s2idle on battery.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/rtc/Kconfig        |  11 +
 drivers/rtc/Makefile       |   1 +
 drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-tad.c | 660 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 672 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/rtc/rtc-qcom-tad.c

diff --git a/drivers/rtc/Kconfig b/drivers/rtc/Kconfig
